WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
- Decisions based on
accounting considerations

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.430
can destroy company value.

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:08.090
In this video I will show you

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:11.160
why you need to understand
the basics of accounting

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.520
in order to be successful in your career.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.560
Hi everyone, my name is Heinrich,

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.410
and welcome to another
episode of Firm Learning.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:21.010
Thank you very much for watching.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.640
If you haven't yet, please
subscribe to the channel below,

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.110
and press the like button

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.070
if you take any value
out of this video at all.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.530
Accounting is something that many people

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:31.950
consider boring,

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.199
and I've seen even many business students

00:00:34.199 --> 00:00:37.090
not taking a large
interest into accounting,

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:40.260
or even completing a
business studies degree,

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.600
or business administration degree,

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.090
and not really having understood
the basics of accounting,

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:49.090
even though they had some
kind of lectures in it.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.110
In my opinion, this is a problem.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.016
And from my experience of having worked

00:00:53.016 --> 00:00:57.000
several years in a large
strategy consulting firm,

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.910
I can assure you that
is absolutely necessary

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:03.780
for your career that you know
the basics of accounting.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:07.180
And the reason is that in
business, on a managerial level,

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:09.317
many decisions need to be made

00:01:09.317 --> 00:01:11.891
in which accounting plays a role.

00:01:11.891 --> 00:01:15.020
Managers actually take decisions

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.101
due to accounting considerations.

00:01:17.101 --> 00:01:20.030
And this can often lead to decisions

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:24.430
that actually destroy company
value just for the reason that

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:28.390
it might positively affect
some accounting figures.

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:31.180
So in order to take part
in these discussions

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:35.100
you need to understand these
accounting considerations

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.200
so that you understand your colleagues

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.260
if they want to go a certain path,

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.730
which might not be driven
by financial value,

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:44.780
but might be driven by an
accounting consideration.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.490
And this is not to say that these people

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:49.410
make these decisions
because they are irrational,

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.000
or because they don't
know what they are doing,

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.849
they have very good reasons
why they choose certain actions

00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:59.464
which might destroy firm
value just due to accounting,

00:01:59.464 --> 00:02:01.950
because of course accounting matters.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.760
If a company publishes accounting figures

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.372
which aren't up to the expectations,

00:02:06.372 --> 00:02:09.343
there can be some real consequences.

00:02:09.343 --> 00:02:12.930
Frequently the compensation of managers

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.260
is closely tied to accounting figures.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:16.570
And these things are real,

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:19.070
companies can overvalue these things,

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:21.680
over actual financial
value of the company.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:25.450
And it is just crucial that
you understand these trade-offs

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.610
in order to be considered
a competent member

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:30.830
in the management group
that you are a part of.

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:32.210
In this video I will give you

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:35.230
two tangible examples of situations,

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:39.376
how accounting can distort
managerial decision makers.

00:02:39.376 --> 00:02:43.190
These are two stylized
situations that actually

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:46.100
you see pretty frequently
happening out there.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:49.160
These things are not just
theoretical examples,

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.560
but really happen all the time,

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.370
I can tell you this from my experience

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.448
in having worked in several industries.

00:02:55.448 --> 00:02:58.550
So it's really crucial that
you understand these examples,

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:02.010
but make no mistake, there are
many, many other situations.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.810
Many, many other accounting driven reasons

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.440
where companies might take a certain route

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.910
which might improve
certain short term figures,

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:13.880
but actually to value
destruction on a financial level.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:15.570
So let's jump into it.

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:17.770
So the first topic is the distinction

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:19.800
between OPEX and CAPEX.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.500
So just the preliminary remark,

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.960
this video cannot serve as
a full accounting primer,

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.200
but I'll try to explain
some basic concepts

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:29.710
just as a refresher.

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:31.590
But if you're completely
new to accounting,

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:33.981
if you did not have any
training in this topic at all,

00:03:33.981 --> 00:03:37.140
please look out for some
more basic resources.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.700
This video will assume that you at least

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.940
have a basic understanding
of how accounting works.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.030
So the first example of what kind

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.490
of accounting considerations
can actually destroy

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:50.524
a company value, is the
distinction between OPEX and CAPEX.

00:03:50.524 --> 00:03:52.530
So, OPEX and CAPEX, what is this?

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:55.300
Here I have the definition of Wikipedia.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:57.810
OPEX stands for Operating Expenditure.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:00.240
It's defined as an
ongoing cost for running

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.410
a product, business, or system.

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:05.480
So this is just the regular
cost that companies have.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.770
So you can consider, for
instance, personal costs here.

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:11.735
You can consider here costs
for service providers,

00:04:11.735 --> 00:04:14.040
for rent that you need to pay

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:15.550
for the facilities that you run,

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:19.510
for utilities, for all
these kinds of things,

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:22.120
for business supplies,
just the other costs

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.780
of just running a business,
of running a product.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:27.830
So this is considered OPEX,
operating expenditure.

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:30.340
And OPEX is often contrasted by CAPEX.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:33.280
CAPEX stands for a capital expenditure,

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.810
and it is defined as
the cost of developing

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:40.810
or providing non-consumable
parts for the product or system.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.670
And here are some examples,

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:44.920
this is something that
you would usually consider

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:47.590
an investment, an investment to something.

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:49.500
So for instance buying a machine,

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.218
that then you use to produce products.

00:04:52.218 --> 00:04:55.850
Buying a building, buying some land,

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:58.080
that you use to build a factory.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.561
So things where you invest a
certain amount of money today,

00:05:00.561 --> 00:05:03.290
for which you hope that
you will get returns.

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:06.370
So that you will hope to get
some useful value out of it

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:09.300
in the future, over a
longer period of time.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:10.770
So why does this matter?

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:14.304
How is this distinction at
all relevant to managers

00:05:14.304 --> 00:05:17.123
making decisions for their companies.

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:20.974
Let's look into how OPEX
and CAPEX expenditures

00:05:20.974 --> 00:05:24.820
affect the income statement
and the balance statement.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:27.040
And for starters, in one dimension of cost

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.220
they're completely the same in the sense

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:31.220
that you need to pay now, right?

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:33.370
From a cash flow perspective,

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.330
it does not make any difference

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:37.630
if you have an OPEX expenditure,

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:40.160
or a CAPEX expenditure, right?

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.060
So if you pay for CAPEX or for OPEX,

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:44.230
you need to pay now.

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:46.720
So this doesn't have to do anything here,

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:48.420
this is the same for both.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.230
But there are differences when it comes

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:53.180
to the income statement,
and the balance sheet.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:54.800
And now let's look at the two.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:57.150
OPEX operating expenditure

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:59.740
directly goes into the income statement,

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:01.680
into the profit and loss statement.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:03.850
So if you spend a certain amount of money,

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:06.579
for instance, for your
personnel, for you employees,

00:06:06.579 --> 00:06:09.330
then you will put that an as expense

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:11.210
in your income statement,

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:15.200
in the exact year that
the expense has occurred.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:17.163
And the implication of that is that

00:06:17.163 --> 00:06:18.917
if you will pay now, for instance,

00:06:18.917 --> 00:06:22.240
€100, $100 to pay your personnel,

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:23.570
or to buy office equipment,

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:26.961
or on other expenditures
which are considered OPEX,

00:06:26.961 --> 00:06:30.130
then the profit, the earnings before tax,

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:32.850
or then the net profit in the very end

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:34.490
that you will need to report,

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:37.575
will be reduced by the exact amount

00:06:37.575 --> 00:06:40.320
of the expenditure that you had.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:44.320
So OPEX is directly affecting
your income statement,

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:47.756
your P&amp;L, if you spent a dollar in OPEX,

00:06:47.756 --> 00:06:51.510
your profit is directly in that same year

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:53.840
reduced by the same dollar.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:57.178
And this is why managers
often do not like OPEX,

00:06:57.178 --> 00:07:00.870
because it directly
reduces their net profit.

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:04.874
It directly has a negative
impact on their P&amp;L, right?

00:07:04.874 --> 00:07:07.710
So let's now contrast that with CAPEX

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:10.790
with an expense that you can
book as capital expenditure.

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:13.764
So again, from a cash flow
perspective, that's the same,

00:07:13.764 --> 00:07:16.650
you need to pay directly right now

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:18.781
all the money leaves your bank account.

00:07:18.781 --> 00:07:21.690
But the difference is that it does not

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:23.954
directly go through your income statement,

00:07:23.954 --> 00:07:26.820
because it first gets
into your balance sheet.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:30.149
So let's imagine now for
€400 or €100 million,

00:07:30.149 --> 00:07:34.410
or whatever the amount
may be, you buy a machine.

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:37.009
Now, in your assets on the balance sheet,

00:07:37.009 --> 00:07:39.460
there will be a machine appearing

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:44.140
with the book value of
€100 million or dollar,

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:46.880
but it does not directly
go to the income statement.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:49.358
So you do not have to
directly book an expense

00:07:49.358 --> 00:07:53.070
of 100 million in your income statement.

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:54.550
So what happens instead,

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:57.360
if you incur a CAPEX expense,

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:59.720
there is a depreciation schedule.

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:02.020
So there's decided that each year

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:04.688
over a certain defined amount of years,

00:08:04.688 --> 00:08:08.762
a certain portion of the CAPEX
expense that you incurred

00:08:08.762 --> 00:08:12.514
will go to the income
statement as depreciation.

00:08:12.514 --> 00:08:16.394
So, if you have now this
machine of worth 100 million,

00:08:16.394 --> 00:08:19.320
there might be maybe a 20 year

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.560
or a 50 year depreciation period.

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:26.750
So, if now you have a 20
year depreciation period,

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:30.651
this means that each year for 20 years,

00:08:30.651 --> 00:08:35.651
you will pay €5 million
or dollar as depreciation

00:08:35.816 --> 00:08:37.570
in your income statement.

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:41.390
So, instead of taking the
direct hit of 100 right now,

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:44.491
in this year, you suddenly
only need to show five

00:08:44.491 --> 00:08:47.232
as the depreciation in this year.

00:08:47.232 --> 00:08:50.440
And of course, this has a much better

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:53.060
much more favorable net profit effect,

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:54.670
especially in the short term.

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:56.190
So what happens the next,

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:58.300
then of course, the asset in the next year

00:08:58.300 --> 00:08:59.977
gets reduced by the depreciation.

00:08:59.977 --> 00:09:02.990
So if you depreciate it by 5 million,

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:05.129
then instead of 100 on your balance sheet,

00:09:05.129 --> 00:09:07.803
it will only show five, next year,

00:09:07.803 --> 00:09:09.830
and so on and so forth.

00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:12.650
So again, what does
this mean for managers?

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:15.130
And the truth is that
even though of course,

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:18.480
there are accounting rules which prevent

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:20.490
you to freely choosing from,

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:23.330
whether you book something
as OPEX or CAPEX.

00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:26.920
In reality, you often have
some degrees of freedom,

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:29.410
whether you book something
as OPEX or CAPEX.

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:32.300
So here you could be in a
situation where you decide

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:33.710
how you book something,

00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:36.556
or maybe you have to choose
between two projects,

00:09:36.556 --> 00:09:39.670
and the one project you can
book and expense as OPEX

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:41.630
and then the other project

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:44.890
you can book as an expense as CAPEX.

00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:47.510
So, let's look now over
a longer period of time,

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:48.730
how the decision whether

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:51.080
you have an OPEX or CAPEX expenditure,

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:54.750
actually affects your income statement.

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:56.477
So the first option is OPEX.

00:09:56.477 --> 00:09:59.140
Let's imagine our company that earns

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:02.207
for a period of 10 years, revenues of 50,

00:10:02.207 --> 00:10:05.619
and in order to earn these revenues of 50,

00:10:05.619 --> 00:10:10.380
they need to incur an OPEX expense of 100.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:12.850
And let's imagine just for
the sake of the argument

00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:15.700
that there are no other
expenses in this company.

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:18.410
So this would lead to the
situation in the first year,

00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:20.930
you would show a profit of minus 50.

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:22.190
And then in the next years,

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:24.530
you would show a profit of plus 50

00:10:24.530 --> 00:10:27.750
because the OPEX directly
gets P&amp;L effective

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:30.310
in the very first year
and actually incurs,

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:32.155
and then you do not need to book it again

00:10:32.155 --> 00:10:34.780
in the next years after.

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:36.490
So if you sum this up,

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:38.630
you get to a situation
where after 10 years,

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:41.040
you will have made revenues of 500.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.138
So 10 times 15,

00:10:43.138 --> 00:10:45.509
you will have OPEX expenses of 100

00:10:45.509 --> 00:10:48.350
and therefore a total profit of 400.

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:50.220
So let's contrast that with the scenario

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:52.610
where you have a very similar situation,

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:55.625
but instead of booking
the same expenses as OPEX,

00:10:55.625 --> 00:10:57.970
you are actually able to book it as CAPEX,

00:10:57.970 --> 00:11:00.030
and now the situation
looks a bit different

00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:01.998
in the sense that, in the first year,

00:11:01.998 --> 00:11:05.250
if you assume that the
CAPEX gets depreciated

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:07.360
over the full 10 year lifespan

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:09.300
of this project of this company,

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:12.050
you only need to pay 10 every year.

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:15.357
It's 100 divided by 10,
so the result is 10.

00:11:15.357 --> 00:11:19.184
And the result now is
that at the same revenues,

00:11:19.184 --> 00:11:22.440
depreciation 10 in the very first year,

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:25.990
instead of needing to
show a profit of minus 50,

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:28.787
you can now show a profit of plus 40.

00:11:28.787 --> 00:11:30.650
But then of course, the downside is that

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:33.980
in the next years, you have
the same depreciation amounts,

00:11:33.980 --> 00:11:36.566
so we already showed the 40 as a profit.

00:11:36.566 --> 00:11:38.050
And as you can imagine,

00:11:38.050 --> 00:11:41.559
many managers will highly
benefit the situation

00:11:41.559 --> 00:11:43.250
of the CAPEX investment

00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:46.320
over the situation of the OPEX investment,

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:50.190
because they do not want
to show a loss of 50

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:51.540
in the first year.

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:54.414
And there's many also empirical
studies that investors

00:11:54.414 --> 00:11:57.540
dislike lots of uncertainty,

00:11:57.540 --> 00:11:59.334
what the earnings of a company are.

00:11:59.334 --> 00:12:01.727
So it's often considered favorably,

00:12:01.727 --> 00:12:04.830
if you can smoothen out
the earnings over time,

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:06.920
compared to having a situation

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:08.940
where you have high jumps

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:10.878
and suddenly being significantly negative

00:12:10.878 --> 00:12:13.060
in the earnings of the company.

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:15.280
Of course, the second effect is that

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:19.350
the compensation of managers
is often tied to the earnings.

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:21.730
And you can imagine that
the personal compensation

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:24.033
of a manager alone, it's
significantly better

00:12:24.033 --> 00:12:29.033
if the company shows a profit
of 40, compared to minus 50.

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:31.888
And people tend to discount the future

00:12:31.888 --> 00:12:34.120
in favor of the short term.

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:36.610
Maybe also, because this
person only plans to stay

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:38.436
at the company for a
certain amount of years,

00:12:38.436 --> 00:12:41.967
which is why CAPEX investments
often gets favored.

00:12:41.967 --> 00:12:43.131
And of course,

00:12:43.131 --> 00:12:47.180
if you add all of this up
over the 10 year time period,

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:50.000
you see that the revenues
are exactly the same.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:52.870
The expenses are in the
long run exactly the same

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:56.270
not only that, it's booked as
depreciation instead of OPEX.

00:12:56.270 --> 00:12:58.030
And the profit is exactly the same.

00:12:58.030 --> 00:13:01.190
But you will see that managers
favor CAPEX investment

00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:04.240
for the OPEX expenditure
for these reasons.

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:07.890
So why can this destroy
financial value for the company?

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:10.450
Well imagine that now
you would have project

00:13:10.450 --> 00:13:12.790
with the payoff profile of this option one

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:14.300
in front of you as a manager,

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:17.597
and you would be able to
earn revenues of in total 500

00:13:17.597 --> 00:13:21.978
over a 10 year period, by
investing once, OPEX 100.

00:13:21.978 --> 00:13:24.668
Of course, on the financial
value perspective,

00:13:24.668 --> 00:13:27.060
it would be beneficial for the company

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:29.830
to undergo this investment project.

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:33.200
But now if you would be strongly
incentivized as a manager

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:34.490
for the short term,

00:13:34.490 --> 00:13:37.270
or maybe you are in a bit of
a tough financial situation

00:13:37.270 --> 00:13:38.300
with the company.

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:39.931
You are under financial distress,

00:13:39.931 --> 00:13:42.140
you may be in a restructuring process

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:44.669
and have committed to
certain profit numbers

00:13:44.669 --> 00:13:47.338
to your external stakeholders
and to investors.

00:13:47.338 --> 00:13:50.813
You might decide to
not do this option one,

00:13:50.813 --> 00:13:53.959
even though it would make
financial sense for the company

00:13:53.959 --> 00:13:56.644
due to the short term negative effects

00:13:56.644 --> 00:13:59.670
in your books in your
accounting statements

00:13:59.670 --> 00:14:01.330
that this project might have.

00:14:01.330 --> 00:14:04.880
So now let's look into a
second type of examples.

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:06.707
And the second type of situations

00:14:06.707 --> 00:14:09.280
that I've seen many times occur,

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:11.830
especially in a retail context,

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:14.110
but other industries as well.

00:14:14.110 --> 00:14:18.280
And this is the effect of
which depreciation schedule

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:22.219
exactly you have on your
inventory, on your assets,

00:14:22.219 --> 00:14:25.800
and how that depreciation
schedule might affect

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:28.485
your decision to sell or not to sell

00:14:28.485 --> 00:14:30.462
certain products in the future.

00:14:30.462 --> 00:14:33.470
So let's think about
the following scenario.

00:14:33.470 --> 00:14:35.860
You are a buyer of a retail company

00:14:35.860 --> 00:14:38.687
let's say you are a fashion
and apparel company,

00:14:38.687 --> 00:14:41.589
and you go out there and you buy 50 units

00:14:41.589 --> 00:14:44.000
of certain merchandise.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:47.170
And you know that you can
sell the units in this year

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:50.170
at a retail price of five euro per unit,

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:54.980
you know that this is the fair
market price of these pieces.

00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:58.660
And now you're able to buy
these at a value of two.

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:00.560
So sounds like good deal, right?

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:03.410
You can sell it at five and buy it two.

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:05.130
So now what does the balance sheet

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:07.630
at the beginning of this year look like?

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:11.200
You have an inventory of
100 in the balance sheet

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:13.170
because you bought 50 units

00:15:13.170 --> 00:15:15.690
at a book value, at a
purchase price of two.

00:15:15.690 --> 00:15:17.820
So let's just assume for simplicity

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:20.172
that purchase price equals the book value.

00:15:20.172 --> 00:15:23.058
And now you go out there
in the marketplace,

00:15:23.058 --> 00:15:25.890
and you start now to sell these things.

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:27.080
And in the first year,

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:30.360
you achieve to say 20
units of the 50 units

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:31.970
that you initially purchase.

00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:34.711
And this for an apparent
for retail company

00:15:34.711 --> 00:15:36.960
is nothing too unusual

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:38.955
so you often buy a
certain amount of stock,

00:15:38.955 --> 00:15:41.900
and then of course, you try
to sell as much as possible

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:42.771
as fast as possible,

00:15:42.771 --> 00:15:44.900
but after the season of course

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:47.600
often you have some stocks left over,

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:50.188
then you need to sell
again in the next season.

00:15:50.188 --> 00:15:52.710
And because you know that
you get a retail value

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:54.220
of five per unit,

00:15:54.220 --> 00:15:57.890
these 20 units now in
your income statement

00:15:57.890 --> 00:16:00.700
lead to a revenue of 100.

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:02.350
Now, if you look into the cost,

00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:04.580
let's now go into the income statement

00:16:04.580 --> 00:16:07.010
for these 100 revenues that you made.

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:08.790
This is the cost of goods sold.

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:10.760
This is now a cost of 40.

00:16:10.760 --> 00:16:11.593
Why 40?

00:16:11.593 --> 00:16:14.500
Because here now you need to
look at the book value, right?

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:16.250
You sell 20 units,

00:16:16.250 --> 00:16:19.153
these units are in your book
at a purchase price of two

00:16:19.153 --> 00:16:21.400
so book value of two per unit.

00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:24.030
So two times 20 is 40.

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:27.107
So you need to book costs
of goods sold of €40,

00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:30.310
and this then would
lead to a profit of 60.

00:16:30.310 --> 00:16:32.920
So 100 minus 40.

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:35.660
So what is now the situation in year two?

00:16:35.660 --> 00:16:38.540
Now you have 30 units left in stock,

00:16:38.540 --> 00:16:41.530
because you already
sold 20 of the 50 units

00:16:41.530 --> 00:16:43.100
that you initially have.

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:46.983
And now of course the retail
value of these units decreased

00:16:46.983 --> 00:16:49.870
because usually the longer you wait

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:52.080
and the older your merchandise is,

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:54.571
the price that you actually
get for the merchandise

00:16:54.571 --> 00:16:56.300
is smaller and smaller.

00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:58.930
And again, you think
about a fashion company

00:16:58.930 --> 00:17:00.050
this is very much true.

00:17:00.050 --> 00:17:01.875
In the next season, you will
usually only be able to sell

00:17:01.875 --> 00:17:06.200
the pieces of last season
at a significant discount.

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:09.160
So let's now imagine for
this calculator example

00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:13.288
that instead of getting
five, you can only get three.

00:17:13.288 --> 00:17:15.490
Now let's look into the inventory.

00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:18.020
And here know it gets interesting.

00:17:18.020 --> 00:17:21.203
Because in this example
now the company decided

00:17:21.203 --> 00:17:26.203
to not depreciate the inventory
that they have, right?

00:17:26.970 --> 00:17:29.700
So they still decided to keep a book value

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:32.720
of two per unit on the inventory.

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:34.570
And why would they do that?

00:17:34.570 --> 00:17:36.860
Well, if they do decide to depreciate

00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:38.490
the inventory that they have,

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:41.529
then they would need to
show a depreciation expense

00:17:41.529 --> 00:17:45.150
here in the last year
in the income statement,

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:47.880
in the same height that
they actually depreciate

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:49.960
the inventory that's left over.

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:50.793
And of course,

00:17:50.793 --> 00:17:53.650
for the reasons that we've
seen in the last example,

00:17:53.650 --> 00:17:55.629
the company usually has an incentive

00:17:55.629 --> 00:17:59.460
to depreciate as slowly as possible.

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:01.610
Because the faster you depreciate,

00:18:01.610 --> 00:18:05.754
the quicker the expenses show
up in your income statement

00:18:05.754 --> 00:18:10.290
and as said, you usually
want to delay the expenses

00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:13.107
to hit your income statement
as long as possible,

00:18:13.107 --> 00:18:16.560
in order to show a positive
result for your company

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:18.840
that is as high as possible.

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:22.670
And again companies usually
have some degrees of discretion,

00:18:22.670 --> 00:18:24.941
some degrees of freedom to what extent

00:18:24.941 --> 00:18:27.710
they depreciate the merchandise.

00:18:27.710 --> 00:18:30.537
And here now in this of
course, a bit extreme example,

00:18:30.537 --> 00:18:32.317
we will be in a situation

00:18:32.317 --> 00:18:34.620
where they do not depreciate at all

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:36.730
and you would still keep the merchandise,

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:39.740
those 30 units of stock
at the book value of two

00:18:39.740 --> 00:18:41.400
with the inventory of 60.

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:43.750
So because now it's
already the next season,

00:18:43.750 --> 00:18:47.080
you cannot sell as much
of it as the last season.

00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:50.370
Because usually now you will
already have new merchandise in

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:53.020
which of course try to
sell it with a focus,

00:18:53.020 --> 00:18:55.940
but you still manage now to sell 10 units

00:18:55.940 --> 00:18:58.260
of this overload of inventory.

00:18:58.260 --> 00:19:00.740
So 10 units at a retail value of three

00:19:00.740 --> 00:19:02.590
leads to revenues of 30.

00:19:02.590 --> 00:19:05.647
And for these 10 units,
now you need to book

00:19:05.647 --> 00:19:08.620
€20 of cost of goods sold,

00:19:08.620 --> 00:19:12.000
because they're still in your
books at two euro per unit.

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:13.689
So 10 times two is 20.

00:19:13.689 --> 00:19:17.540
Now you would at least
then show profit of 10.

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:19.330
So let's now look into the next year.

00:19:19.330 --> 00:19:22.410
Now you still have 20 units left in stock.

00:19:22.410 --> 00:19:24.540
And the retail value of these 20 units

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:27.470
though keeps of course
decreasing every year,

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:30.270
and now is only at two euro.

00:19:30.270 --> 00:19:31.657
Now what does the inventory look like

00:19:31.657 --> 00:19:34.650
you still did not take
any depreciation expense

00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:37.370
because you try to delay
that as much as possible.

00:19:37.370 --> 00:19:41.341
And now you're 20 units that
you still have an inventory

00:19:41.341 --> 00:19:45.280
of €40 because they bought
that two euro per unit.

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:48.954
And if you now again managed
to sell 10 units of the stock,

00:19:48.954 --> 00:19:53.840
you have no revenues of
20 because 10 times two,

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:56.327
but now you also need to book
cost of goods sold at of two,

00:19:56.327 --> 00:19:59.520
and this then leads to profit of zero.

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:02.730
Now of course the whole
situation doesn't seem

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:04.150
that positive anymore.

00:20:04.150 --> 00:20:05.250
And you seem to ask,

00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:07.180
why do I even keep bothering selling this

00:20:07.180 --> 00:20:09.280
if it doesn't give me any profit.

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:12.160
And now, of course, when
it really gets unpleasant,

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:14.770
is not if you think this
is one year further,

00:20:14.770 --> 00:20:16.860
you stood up the 10 units left,

00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:19.340
they are now only at a retail rate of one.

00:20:19.340 --> 00:20:21.690
So you can only get one euro for it

00:20:21.690 --> 00:20:24.184
because it's not really old merchandise,

00:20:24.184 --> 00:20:26.290
probably already looks a bit old

00:20:26.290 --> 00:20:27.820
and not as fresh anymore,

00:20:27.820 --> 00:20:29.230
and you will only be able to sell it

00:20:29.230 --> 00:20:32.380
at a significant discount.

00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:34.500
But unfortunately, you still have

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:37.673
these 10 pieces in your
books at a value of two,

00:20:37.673 --> 00:20:40.680
because you just did not
want to depreciate it,

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:42.630
because then you would
have taken these sets

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:45.770
in your P&amp;L, in your income
statement much earlier.

00:20:45.770 --> 00:20:48.260
And if you now even would
have the opportunity

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:51.427
to sell these 10 units
at the retail value,

00:20:51.427 --> 00:20:54.410
then you would have to
book revenues of 10.

00:20:54.410 --> 00:20:57.559
But your cost of goods
sold would still be 20.

00:20:57.559 --> 00:21:01.570
And then you would report
a loss for the sale of 10.

00:21:01.570 --> 00:21:04.700
And this is now where managerial decisions

00:21:04.700 --> 00:21:06.750
really can come into play.

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:09.400
And this will be a situation
where some companies

00:21:09.400 --> 00:21:13.370
will prefer to not sell
this inventory at all,

00:21:13.370 --> 00:21:15.950
they will prefer to just keep it somewhere

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:18.850
in your stock to let it rot away,

00:21:18.850 --> 00:21:21.510
but not sell it because if you sell it,

00:21:21.510 --> 00:21:24.170
you would report a loss of 10.

00:21:24.170 --> 00:21:27.030
And you do not want a negative result

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:28.780
in your income statement,

00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:31.520
because the company might
already be stretched,

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:34.330
because the company
might already fear that

00:21:34.330 --> 00:21:36.300
it will not reach the yearly targets.

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:39.612
So they rather don't take the sale at all,

00:21:39.612 --> 00:21:41.750
instead of needing to take the hit

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:43.490
of taking a negative loss of 10.

00:21:43.490 --> 00:21:44.740
And this happens, believe me,

00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:48.040
I've seen things like
that very, very frequently

00:21:48.040 --> 00:21:49.176
in the corporate world.

00:21:49.176 --> 00:21:53.200
People do not want negative
resides on the income statement,

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:55.866
and they would rather
decide to just let it rot

00:21:55.866 --> 00:21:58.610
instead of taking the loss of 10.

00:21:58.610 --> 00:22:01.250
And of course, this is now a situation

00:22:01.250 --> 00:22:04.150
where you really destroy company value.

00:22:04.150 --> 00:22:05.740
Because even though of course,

00:22:05.740 --> 00:22:08.950
you would report a loss of
10 in your income statement,

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:10.910
you would at least still get the money,

00:22:10.910 --> 00:22:13.830
you would at least still
be able to get your €10

00:22:13.830 --> 00:22:15.340
for the merchandise.

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:16.890
But if you do not sell it,

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:20.960
and let it just stay in your
stock pretty much forever,

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:23.190
then the situation is that
you will never say this.

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:25.330
And of course, if you
know wait even longer,

00:22:25.330 --> 00:22:27.811
if you wait even more
years, then at some point,

00:22:27.811 --> 00:22:29.600
you will not be able to sell it at all

00:22:29.600 --> 00:22:32.460
or maybe not at a value
of one euro per unit,

00:22:32.460 --> 00:22:34.850
maybe only at ¢10 per unit.

00:22:34.850 --> 00:22:38.200
So you would have the opportunity
to now get €10 for it,

00:22:38.200 --> 00:22:39.980
but you do not sell it because

00:22:39.980 --> 00:22:43.150
you do not want the income statement loss,

00:22:43.150 --> 00:22:46.100
even though your company
would be better off,

00:22:46.100 --> 00:22:47.770
your company's financial value

00:22:47.770 --> 00:22:49.530
would increase if you sell it.

00:22:49.530 --> 00:22:53.630
So this is managerial decision
making driven by accounting

00:22:53.630 --> 00:22:55.400
that really leads to destruction

00:22:55.400 --> 00:22:57.580
of company value or firm value.

00:22:57.580 --> 00:23:00.260
And this happens, things like that happen

00:23:00.260 --> 00:23:02.820
frequently in the corporate world,

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:06.284
if you do not understand these
accounting considerations,

00:23:06.284 --> 00:23:09.919
you cannot take part in
these managerial discussions.

00:23:09.919 --> 00:23:12.990
So these were the two examples
that I wanted to show you.

00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:15.020
And the reason again,
why I'm showing you this

00:23:15.020 --> 00:23:17.850
is because I want to
stress how important it is

00:23:17.850 --> 00:23:20.690
that you have at least
a basic understanding

00:23:20.690 --> 00:23:23.330
of these kind of
accounting considerations,

00:23:23.330 --> 00:23:26.090
even with business graduates
are often get the feeling

00:23:26.090 --> 00:23:28.430
that many believe that as a manager,

00:23:28.430 --> 00:23:30.810
they will make the important decisions,

00:23:30.810 --> 00:23:33.870
they will think about
what's best for the company.

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:36.160
And then there are some
bookkeepers somewhere

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:37.880
to just take care of the books

00:23:37.880 --> 00:23:40.120
and we'll just make sure
that some of all of this

00:23:40.120 --> 00:23:42.340
gets translated in the correct way.

00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:45.500
But I can tell you this
is a clear misconception.

00:23:45.500 --> 00:23:48.560
On the sea level, large
blue chip companies,

00:23:48.560 --> 00:23:51.593
you can be sure that the senior
leaders of these companies

00:23:51.593 --> 00:23:54.850
have a very good understanding of what

00:23:54.850 --> 00:23:56.890
the accounting implications are

00:23:56.890 --> 00:23:58.500
of the decisions that they take.

00:23:58.500 --> 00:24:00.810
And these things are very much

00:24:00.810 --> 00:24:02.950
top of mind for these companies.

00:24:02.950 --> 00:24:05.060
And if you just enter a discussion,

00:24:05.060 --> 00:24:08.026
just ignoring these
accounting implementations,

00:24:08.026 --> 00:24:09.770
you will look like a fool,

00:24:09.770 --> 00:24:11.970
you will look like you do not understand

00:24:11.970 --> 00:24:14.191
what matters to these
sea level executives.

00:24:14.191 --> 00:24:17.860
So make sure that you get
some accounting basics.

00:24:17.860 --> 00:24:19.947
In my university studies,
there was one book

00:24:19.947 --> 00:24:22.510
which I found really useful

00:24:22.510 --> 00:24:25.270
in learning exactly these
basics of accounting,

00:24:25.270 --> 00:24:28.610
I left the link to the book
below in the description.

00:24:28.610 --> 00:24:30.580
To be fair, it's a bit expensive,

00:24:30.580 --> 00:24:32.330
but I can tell you it's really worth it.

00:24:32.330 --> 00:24:34.560
I bought it myself when I was a student.

00:24:34.560 --> 00:24:37.000
So if you would like a basic primer

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:40.150
into how accounting works,
it's really easy to understand,

00:24:40.150 --> 00:24:42.200
it goes into some level of detail.

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:44.530
And I promise you that
your level of understanding

00:24:44.530 --> 00:24:47.230
will be much higher than
that of most of people.

00:24:47.230 --> 00:24:48.160
And it's also written

00:24:48.160 --> 00:24:50.260
in a fairly easy to understand language.

00:24:50.260 --> 00:24:52.640
So even a few so far did not have

00:24:52.640 --> 00:24:56.010
much exposure of accounting,
this will help you.

00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:57.710
So thank you again for watching.

00:24:57.710 --> 00:25:00.220
I hope you took some
value out of this video.

